  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1205 AM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nUpper trough currently moving into the Great Basin region with\nenergy diving down the west side which will help cutoff a low\nwhich will move into SW AZ by Sat morning. Do not see much\ndifference in the upper pattern across the CWA today into Sat. SSW\nflow aloft with a pseudo dryline setup near the RGV will bring\nscattered to widespread showers and thunderstorms late afternoon\nand evening to eastern zones. Plenty of shear, mostly speed and\ninstability of around 500-1500J/kg CAPE. Current thinking is the \ntornado threat is minimal with higher LCL's and not much low level\nbacking. There will be a significant hail and wind threat into the\nmid evening mainly RGV east. This setup will be nearly the same\nfor Saturday. It does look like a better chance for some multiple\nrounds of thunderstorms Sat and there will be a higher chance for\nflooding, but storms will still be moving quickly. May need a\nFlood Watch, but will hold off for now. \n\nHeading into Sun/Mon, the upper low will slowly open up as it\nmoves across the central Rockies. This will keep a weak upper\ntrough lingering around the area and we keep some ESE surface\nwinds over southeastern CWA. NBM PoPs of slight chance near RGV to\nlikely east looks good. Could still see some severe storms these\ndays but shear will not be as impressive, but pretty good\ninstability remains. Temperatures will still remain about 5 \ndegrees above normal during this time. \n\nAnother trough starts to approach the West Coast Wed-Thu and our\nlow level winds again to S to SE and tap some good moisture.\nChance PoPs return areawide by the end of the period. Temperatures\nwill continue to remain near to slightly above normal.",
